Z||000006||ChadMan||05-10-99||06:46 AM||||I would think that with "homemade" motor mounts and adapter plate between your Buick V8 and tranny, some flywheel work (?), and a 4-core radiator from v8archie, you'd be all set!<p>Even with some hi-po goodies, I doubt your Buick V8 would run with the SB Chevy or Northstar conversions.  Still, if you show up at a national FOCOA meet with your Buick V8 conversion, I bet you'd leave with a LOT of new friends and a "Best Engineered" trophy.  Good luck, Roy!
Z||000007||spike||05-10-99||11:14 AM||||I was wondering about the Northstar engine swap. I did some research on the motors which were availiable in the older (68-70) Cadillac Eldorodos & Tornodos. It seems that some of these cars were equiped with a v8 engine matted to a fwd tranny. The 1970 Eldorado has a 8.2 litre in it, 427 cid bored out to a 500 cid, it produced 400hp & 550 lbs torgue, I wonder if this would fit in a Fiero bay without any major mods such as fram cutting. There would be no need for a tranny adaptor kit. I am trying to find the cheapest why to install a v8 in a Fiero. What do you guys think.
Z||000008||Carrolles||05-11-99||10:54 AM||||If I remember correctly the Aluminum block V-8 is around 215 cubic inches and puts out a little over 200hp.  I helped put one in a Henry-J many years ago.<br>Try V-8 Archie-s site for possible conversion kits or advice.  Also try Gary Zummalt.<p>I was at the Fiero Factory Swap meet in Huntsville Al and saw a Northstar already mounted to a cradle ready for installation for $5500.00.  I believe it had jonly about 100 miles on it.<p>Carroll
Z||000009||spike||06-22-99||07:27 AM||||Roy, I was wondering what you ended up doing with that Buick Al. V8. Did you decide to install it and what happened.
Z||000010||GREENDANO||06-22-99||07:10 PM||||Spike,<br>  The cad motors were mounted longitudinally, while Fiero motors are transverse.  It would take major mods to make it fit.  I saw spy photos in Kit Car Magazine last fall of a Lambo kit that was being mounted on a full tube fram with a 500ci Cadillac motor with a THM 425 transmission.(that's the one that is still FWD but mounts the engine longitudinally)  The fabricator....none other than THE Rick Doberton.  Hot rod and Pro Street guys know who he is.  Two of his cars, the Pontiac J2000 pro streeter and his older Chevy Nova are on display at the local Pontiac dealer by me.  Very cool cars!!!<br>  Dan-O
Z||000011||spike||06-28-99||12:08 PM||||I have done some research on this engine and they claim that it is very easy to get 277 hp from this engine. The weight is 285lbs complete. Is there a fwd tranny that will bolt to this engine.
Z||000012||spike||06-28-99||12:09 PM||||I have done some research on this engine and they claim that it is very easy to get 277 hp from this engine. The weight is 285lbs complete. Is there a fwd tranny that will bolt to this engine.
Z||000013||terryk||08-08-99||01:10 PM||||Forget the AL engine.<p>All V8 swaps except the Northstar require major cutting. I have yet to see one that was appealing.<p>I still think a turbo (either a iron head or Gen II engine version) is the best bang for the buck.<p>The 3.8L swaps are next.
